WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.911
After the emotionally draining epic that was
Avengers: Endgame, Spider-Man: Far From Home

00:00:05.911 --> 00:00:10.800
swung into theaters to offer some sweet relief
to the legions of Marvel fans looking for

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.220
a superhero fix with slightly smaller stakes.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:19.520
Far From Home delivered all the heart, thrills,
and goofiness we've come to expect since Tom

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.280
Holland took over the spandex in 2016's Captain
America: Civil War.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.420
But there are a lot of little touches in Far
From Home you probably missed.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:34.240
A mighty Earth Elemental wreaks some serious
havoc in Mexico during the cold opening of

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.649
Far From Home, so we don't blame you if didn't
catch the super-nerdy clue the filmmakers

00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:41.060
briefly put on screen to herald its arrival.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:47.120
A license plate with only "463" visible appears
briefly near Nick Fury and Maria Hill when

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.300
the Elemental pops up.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:55.269
This is likely a nod to Amazing Spider-Man
#4 from September 1963, the issue that introduced

00:00:55.269 --> 00:00:57.900
the world to the Earth-based villain Sandman.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.360
Sound like a stretch?

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.120
Well, Marvel already snuck a few license-plate-based
Easter eggs into the Far From Home trailers

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.400
that didn't make the theatrical cut of the
film.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.420
Before the water Elemental attack is teased
in the first "Official Teaser Trailer" from

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:17.620
January 2019, for example, there was a boat
in the background with "ASM 212" on the side.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.340
It's almost certainly a reference to Amazing
Spider-Man #212, the issue that introduced

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.200
Spidey's "wet threat": Hydro-Man.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:31.000
Flash Thompson even tells his classmates about
a guy who got water powers named "Morris Bench"

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:35.439
that he read about on the Internet another
clear reference to the comic book alter-ego

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.620
of Hydro-Man.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.260
But that's not all!

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.420
In a London-based clip seen in the "Official
Trailer" from May 2019, a car with the license

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.960
plate "2865 SEP" appears.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:52.789
This probably refers to Amazing Spider-Man
#28 from September 1965, which marked the

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:54.680
debut of the fire villain Molten Man.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:00.020
This particular UK plate was cut from the
final film, but a similar plate number pops

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:07.820
up in Prague in the theatrical cut: ASM 28965,
for Issue #28, September 1965.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.860
There's also at least one other license-plate
based Easter egg in Far From Home that has

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:20.080
nothing to do with the Elementals: MTU 83779,
spotted on Nick Fury's license plate in Berlin.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.530
It may look like just another random string
of letters and numbers, but we're guessing

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:30.930
it's a reference to Marvel Team-Up #83 from
July 1979, which featured a team-up between

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:31.860
Spider-Man and Fury.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:38.260
The story of MTU #83 begins, or so the text
tells us, with a "murdered" Spider-Man on

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:39.680
a Manhattan rooftop.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:40.720
But wait!

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:45.799
He awakens and begins to remember the events
of the night before, which culminated in Nick

00:02:45.799 --> 00:02:51.080
Fury shooting Spider-Man and a woman who appeared
to be Black Widow with an "anaesthetic bullet."

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.400
In other words, he used non-lethal ammo that
appeared real.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:01.220
So, like in the events of the Berlin sequence
of Far From Home, MTU #83 features Fury seemingly

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.960
at odds with our friendly neighborhood Spider-Man.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:08.880
In Far From Home, fake Nick Fury is an illusion
used to trick (and incapacitate) Spider-Man.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:15.540
In MTU #83, Nick Fury's fake bullets are an
illusion used to trick (and incapacitate)

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.060
Spider-Man.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:17.320
Neat!

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.120
The rest of the plot of MTU #83, which involves
Silver Samurai and the teleportation ring

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.520
he obtained from Saturday Night Live's John
Belushi, is probably irrelevant.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.920
It does, however, feature a seemingly dead
Black Widow appearing alive and well in a

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.920
S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:35.300
lab, but we're not even touching that one.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:42.680
To test his newfound powers in his 1962 Amazing
Fantasy #15 debut, Peter Parker takes on a

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:47.970
musclebound wrestler named Crusher Hogan,
who promises $100 to anyone who can stay in

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:50.079
the ring with him for three minutes.

00:03:50.079 --> 00:03:54.959
Our Spider-Man-in-the-making, of course, easily
bests Hogan, and variations on this origin

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.380
story have popped up several times since.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.540
"Kick his spider ass!

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:00.580
Yeah!"

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.040
After Aunt May's "Homeless Support" fundraiser
in Far From Home, if you look closely behind

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.100
Happy, you'll see a Crusher Hogan poster with
that same $100 offer.

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.320
There's also a ring bell nearby, so it looks
like this building is where Crusher made the

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:15.860
offer.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:21.739
We never got to see the MCU's Peter Parker
take Crusher down, but this poster is a decent

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:27.639
consolation prize, and confirmation that MCU
Spidey likely had a similar origin story as

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.140
his comics counterpart.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:35.860
Benjamin Franklin Parker, aka orphan Peter's
father figure "Uncle Ben," has yet to appear

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:38.560
in the MCU, but his memory looms large.

00:04:39.169 --> 00:04:45.039
In the original Amazing Fantasy #15 origin
story, Uncle Ben is killed by an unnamed burglar

00:04:45.039 --> 00:04:49.710
the same burglar Peter could have apprehended
following a TV appearance, had he not been

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:51.660
so caught up in his newfound fame.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:56.640
Peter later tracks the burglar to a warehouse,
where he captures him and leaves him for the

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:57.160
police to deal with.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.470
Peter realizes in the final panel, famously,
that "with great power there must also come

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:04.520
great responsibility!"

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.940
Spider-Man: Homecoming writer John Francis
Daley told Entertainment Weekly in 2017 that

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.750
Uncle Ben was almost referenced directly in
the MCU, but plans changed.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:20.440
"It was when [Peter] was getting ready for
homecoming and the wardrobe [Aunt May] was

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.340
giving Peter was all Uncle Ben’s clothes.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.180
It was a nice moment, but we also knew that
it veered away from his arc.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:30.540
If you’re going to talk about someone’s
death, you don’t want it to be a throwaway."

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.380
In Far From Home, it's revealed that Peter
is using Ben's old luggage, but the connection

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.980
goes unmentioned on screen.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:42.620
If you look closely, the monogram "BFP" is
featured on the suitcase in the scene in which

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:44.420
Peter is packing for his summer vacation.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.180
Blink and you'll miss it, but the giant novelty
check Happy Hogan carries into the back room

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:54.460
after the "Homeless Support" fundraiser in
Far From Home is signed by Pepper Potts.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.920
Potts is listed here as the co-founder and
chair of the "Stark Relief Foundation," which

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.540
probably sounds familiar if you've rewatched
Avengers: Age of Ultron recently.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:06.120
"Stark Relief Foundation?"

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:06.640
"Already on the scene."

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.120
In Age of Ultron, Tony Stark asks Maria Hill
about The Stark Relief Foundation following

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.069
the so-called "Duel of Johannesburg," in which
a spellbound Hulk and a Hulkbuster-suited

00:06:18.069 --> 00:06:22.300
Tony Stark caused massive collateral damage
to South Africa's largest city.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:27.790
The foundation's purpose, it appears, is to
provide aid to populations impacted by the

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:30.300
messy work the Avengers have historically
engaged in.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:35.440
In Far From Home, viewers can assume that
Pepper has donated such a large sum to help

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:38.540
the homeless population of Queens recovering
after "the blip."

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:44.160
The brief-but-welcome return of Daily Bugle
head honcho J. Jonah Jameson, played once

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.810
again by J.K. Simmons, is one of the many
highlights of Far From Home, coming during

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:53.140
the mid-credits sequence which appears to
set up the next MCU Spider-Man flick.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:58.100
Simmons played the cantankerous journalist
in Sam Raimi's Spider-Man trilogy, but Far

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.120
From Home's incarnation is a little bit different.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:00.980
"He must have given up.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:02.760
Thrown in the towel.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.640
Abandoned his sad little masquerade!"

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.620
In Far From Home, Jameson is more unstable
than ever, and his desire to smear Spider-Man

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:14.020
is cranked up to 11.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:20.020
Befitting of the times, the Daily Bugle now
appears to be an online conspiracy-mongering

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:25.380
tabloid news program, clearly styled to resemble
The O'Reilly Factor, or even the MCU's version

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:25.980
of InfoWars.

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:32.740
This darker take on Jameson then outs Spider-Man
as Peter Parker, leading Parker, in Far From

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:37.860
Home's final line, to echo the final interrupted
line of Homecoming, uttered by Aunt May when

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.280
she discovers Peter's secret:

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:47.340
"What the f — "

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:53.440
Considering that ex-Stark Industries scientist-turned-Mysterio
lackey William Ginter Riva preserved Mysterio's

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:58.420
high-tech handiwork for later use, meaning
even more crazily realistic illusions could

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:04.300
be on the way in Far From Home's follow-up,
having a paranoid conspiracy-monger like Jonah

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:08.580
eager to smear a now-outed Peter Parker is
a recipe for disaster.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.400
WTF, indeed.

